### Title
Multichannel behavior with the default `conda-rattler-solver`

### Why
The next release makes `conda-rattler-solver` the default solver. It passes each multichannel (`defaults`, `local`, condarc `custom_multichannels`) to rattler as a priority group, which differs from the classic solver (members ranked separately) and is only complete once conda-rattler-solver#182 lands and a new conda-rattler-solver release is available.

### Platforms
- [ ] Windows
- [ ] macOS
- [ ] Linux

### Prerequisites
- Network access to `repo.anaconda.com` and `conda.anaconda.org`.
- A conda build whose default solver is `conda-rattler-solver` including conda-rattler-solver#182 (py-rattler >=0.27.1). Check with `conda info` (solver `rattler`) and `conda list -n base conda-rattler-solver`.
- Isolate configuration. Replace `<scratch>` with a new empty directory and set for the whole session:
  - Unix: `export CONDARC=<scratch>/condarc CONDA_PKGS_DIRS=<scratch>/pkgs`
  - PowerShell: `$env:CONDARC="<scratch>\condarc"; $env:CONDA_PKGS_DIRS="<scratch>\pkgs"`
- Do not pass `--solver`; the default is what is tested. Every command uses `--override-channels` and a throwaway `--prefix`.
- Expected results below come from reading the conda-rattler-solver#182 source and from repodata checked on 2026-10-30 (`linux-64`, `osx-arm64`, `win-64`). They were not produced by running conda. Where the exact result depends on rattler internals, the step says "record" instead of giving a pass/fail value.

### Steps

#### Part 1: Sanity check (every platform)
1. Create an environment from `defaults`:
   ```console
   conda create --prefix <scratch>/sanity --override-channels --channel defaults python zlib --yes
   conda list --prefix <scratch>/sanity --show-channel-urls
   ```
2. Use the multichannel name and a member name in specs:
   ```console
   conda create --dry-run --override-channels --channel defaults defaults::zlib pkgs/main::sqlite
   ```
3. Update the environment from step 1:
   ```console
   conda update --prefix <scratch>/sanity --override-channels --channel defaults --all --dry-run
   ```
4. Windows only: add `m2-bash` to the step 1 specs. It is served by the `pkgs/msys2` member of `defaults`.
5. Linux and macOS only: solve for Windows, where `defaults` has an extra member:
   ```console
   conda create --dry-run --override-channels --channel defaults --platform win-64 python
   ```
6. Remove the step 1 environment: `conda remove --prefix <scratch>/sanity --all --yes`.

#### Part 2: Several multichannels mixed with standalone channels
Three real channels are used, all fully populated on every platform:
- `pkgs/main` (`https://repo.anaconda.com/pkgs/main`).
- `anaconda` (`https://conda.anaconda.org/anaconda`), a byte-identical mirror of `pkgs/main` (same filenames and sha256).
- `conda-forge`.

Because `pkgs/main` and `anaconda` serve identical packages, only channel priority can decide which one supplies a package, and the answer is visible in the record's channel label. The probes `fzf` and `hicolor-icon-theme` have no dependencies and exist in all three channels.

7. Create `<scratch>/condarc` with exactly this content, then run `conda config --show custom_multichannels` to confirm it loads:
   ```yaml
   channel_priority: strict
   custom_multichannels:
     mirrors:
       - https://repo.anaconda.com/pkgs/main
       - anaconda
     forge:
       - conda-forge
   ```
8. Run each case. For each, note the channel of `fzf` and of `hicolor-icon-theme` from the `LINK` records of the JSON output (the `channel` field):
   ```console
   conda create --dry-run --override-channels <channels> fzf hicolor-icon-theme --json
   ```
   | Case | `<channels>` |
   | --- | --- |
   | M1 | `--channel pkgs/main --channel conda-forge` |
   | M2 | `--channel conda-forge --channel pkgs/main` |
   | M3 | `--channel forge --channel mirrors` |
   | M4 | `--channel mirrors --channel forge` |
   | M5 | `--channel anaconda --channel conda-forge --channel mirrors` |
   | M6 | `--channel mirrors --channel conda-forge --channel anaconda` |
   | M7 | `--channel conda-forge --channel anaconda --channel mirrors` |
   | M8 | `--channel pkgs/main --channel mirrors --channel conda-forge` |
9. Group-qualified and member-qualified specs, with `--channel mirrors --channel forge` on all three:
   ```console
   conda create --dry-run --override-channels --channel mirrors --channel forge mirrors::fzf forge::hicolor-icon-theme --json
   conda create --dry-run --override-channels --channel mirrors --channel forge conda-forge::fzf anaconda::hicolor-icon-theme --json
   conda create --dry-run --override-channels --channel mirrors --channel forge pkgs/main::fzf --json
   ```
10. A dependency available in only one channel. The `conda-forge` build of `sqlite` needs `libsqlite`, which `pkgs/main` does not have:
    ```console
    conda create --dry-run --override-channels --channel conda-forge sqlite --json
    conda create --dry-run --override-channels --channel mirrors --channel forge sqlite --json
    ```
11. Real install and update through the groups:
    ```console
    conda create --prefix <scratch>/mix --override-channels --channel mirrors --channel forge hicolor-icon-theme --yes
    conda install --prefix <scratch>/mix --override-channels --channel forge --channel mirrors fzf --yes
    conda list --prefix <scratch>/mix --show-channel-urls
    conda update --prefix <scratch>/mix --override-channels --channel forge --channel mirrors --all --dry-run
    ```
12. Repeat M1 to M8 with `channel_priority: flexible`, and again with `--solver classic` for comparison.
13. Channel with nothing for the platform. Add `bioconda` (no `win-64` packages) to a group and solve for Windows, either on Windows or with `--platform win-64`:
    ```console
    conda config --add custom_multichannels.sparse pkgs/main
    conda config --add custom_multichannels.sparse bioconda
    conda create --dry-run --override-channels --channel sparse fzf --platform win-64 --json
    ```
15. Remove the environments and unset `CONDARC` and `CONDA_PKGS_DIRS`.

### Pass criteria

Part 1:
- Steps 1 to 3 solve without errors and `conda list --show-channel-urls` shows `pkgs/main` (and `pkgs/r` or `pkgs/msys2` only when a spec requires it).
- Step 2 accepts both `defaults::zlib` and `pkgs/main::sqlite`, with both served from `pkgs/main`. The member name resolves through `defaults` and does not fail with a missing-channel error.
- Step 4 installs `m2-bash` from `pkgs/msys2` on Windows.
- Step 5 solves for `win-64` without a 404 or missing-channel error.

Part 2, firm results (the first-listed channel wins; read the winner from the `channel` field):
- M1: both probes come from `pkgs/main`.
- M2: both probes come from `conda-forge`.
- M3: both probes come from `conda-forge`.
- M4: both probes come from `pkgs/main` or `anaconda` (members of `mirrors` are interchangeable, so either label is acceptable), never from `conda-forge`.
- M5: both probes come from `anaconda`. This is the key regression check: `anaconda` is listed first on its own, and a solver that pulls the whole `mirrors` group forward when one member is listed alone would pick `pkgs/main` instead.
- M6: both probes come from `pkgs/main` or `anaconda`, never from `conda-forge`.
- M7: both probes come from `conda-forge`.
- M8: both probes come from `pkgs/main` or `anaconda`, never from `conda-forge`. `pkgs/main` is a member of `mirrors`, so it may share a group with `anaconda`.
- Step 9: all three commands solve. `conda-forge::fzf` gives the `conda-forge` build (0.74.5) and `pkgs/main::fzf` gives the `pkgs/main` build (0.74.0). `anaconda::hicolor-icon-theme` comes from `anaconda` or `pkgs/main` (0.18).
- Step 10: both commands install `sqlite` together with `libsqlite` from `conda-forge` (the only place it exists), with no unsatisfiable-dependency error.
- Step 11: `conda list --show-channel-urls` shows channel names (not raw URLs and not `<unknown>`), and every package downloads without 404 or authentication errors. The final `--all` update completes and reports a consistent plan.
- Step 13: the solve succeeds. A listed channel with no packages for the platform must not cause an error or change which channel serves `fzf`.
- Step 14 is informational. A successful solve is a pass. Failing with `HTTP 404 Not Found for channel defaults` is the known #15829; any other failure is new and should be reported.

Part 2, record only (do not fail on these, but attach the output to the QA issue):
- Step 8 results for M1 to M8 under `flexible` priority and under `--solver classic` (step 12). Differences from strict, or from the classic solver, are expected; a solve that fails where strict succeeded is not.
- In step 9, which of `pkgs/main` and `anaconda` supplies `mirrors::fzf`. Both are valid because the members share a group.

### Out of scope / notes
- The classic solver ranks group members separately. Differences from it are not regressions.
- Already covered by conda-rattler-solver tests, so not repeated here: group-qualified and build-qualified specs on a single `file://` multichannel, member-first ordering with one custom multichannel, grouped update build floors, authenticated and CDN `base_url` records, and empty multichannels such as `local` without conda-build paths.
- Not covered by those tests, and the reason for Part 2: real channels served over HTTPS, several multichannels at once, a member listed alone while its group is also listed, standalone channels interleaved with groups, `flexible` priority, cross-channel dependencies (`libsqlite`), and a channel with no content for the platform.
- Not tested here because the result depends on rattler internals that were not verified: how rattler orders two multichannels that share members, and CEP-42 `channel_relations` (`base`/`overrides`) around a group.
- `pkgs/r` is not used for probes. It is empty on `osx-arm64` and stale on `win-64`. `pkgs/msys2` exists only for `win-64`. `nvidia` has no `osx-arm64` packages and `pytorch` has large dependency trees, so neither is used.

### Reference data
Checked against repodata on 2026-10-30. `fzf` and `hicolor-icon-theme` have no dependencies and a build for each of `linux-64`, `osx-arm64` and `win-64`. `anaconda` is identical to `pkgs/main` for both.

| Package | `pkgs/main` / `anaconda` | `conda-forge` | Used for |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `fzf` | 0.74.0 | 0.74.5 | Probe; version also shows which channel served it. |
| `hicolor-icon-theme` | 0.18 | 0.17 | Probe; opposite version ordering to `fzf`. |
| `sqlite` | 3.53.4 | 3.54.1 | `conda-forge` build depends on `libsqlite`, which only `conda-forge` has. |
| `m2-bash` | not available | available on `win-64` | Served by `pkgs/msys2` when only `defaults` is used (Windows sanity check). |
